WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.830
CEOs have a reputation of being unempathetic,
profit-driven, corporate pigs.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:11.230
In many cases, CEOs have little care for the
mental and physical health of their employees,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.860
and the only thing they care about is overall
employee output.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.869
Unfortunately, most of the famous CEOs and
founders in the world exhibit many of these

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.679
characteristics, and this has caused many
people to assume that there’s no such thing

00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:27.970
as a good CEO, at least from an employee’s
perspective.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.800
But this is actually not the case.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.879
There are dozens of fortune 500s CEOs that
are cherished by their employees.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:39.460
And Glassdoor regularly makes a list of the
most approved CEOs in the world based on the

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.190
millions of employee reviews on their website.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.340
The executive that tops the list is Shantanu
Narayen, the CEO of Abode who enjoys an approval

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.149
rating of 99%.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:55.480
Now, technically Rich Lesser did edge him
out in 2021, but if we go back to the 2019

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.180
list, we’ll see that Rich Lesser doesn’t
make the top 100 while Shantanu is still number

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.180
5.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.800
I went ahead and did the math and Shantanu
Narayen has the highest average rank since

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:05.800
2019.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.640
So, here’s how Shantanu Narayen became the
CEO of Adobe and skyrocketed the company while

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.770
being an employee favorite.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:20.020
Taking a look back, Shantanu Narayen was born
on May 27, 1962, in Hyderabad, India.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.500
He was born to a pretty well-off family as
his father owned a plastics company and his

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.880
mother was an American literature professor.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.070
Despite his parents high status within society,
Shantanu didn’t really have any massive

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.190
ambitions.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.090
Really, all he wanted to do was become a journalist
and play cricket.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.790
This is actually a super interesting point
as Satya Nadella, the CEO of Microsoft and

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.960
another top-ranking CEO on the glassdoor list,
also didn’t have any massive ambitions.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.160
It seems like there’s an inverse correlation
between ambition and ego and how good the

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.010
individual is as a leader.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.770
Anyway, Shantanu attended a local school called
Hyderabad Public School.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.290
When it came to picking a major, Shantanu
said that the only real options he had were

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.090
engineering and medicine.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.260
And, he was extremely scared of blood, so
he went with engineering.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.560
A few years later, Shantanu graduated from
Osmania University with a degree in electronics

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.390
and communication engineering.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:18.150
He would then turn around and secure a master’s
in computer science from Bowling Green State

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.260
University in Ohio in 1986.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.230
After completing his master’s, Shantanu
jumped into the workforce scoring a job at

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.230
Measurex Automation Systems.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:32.319
He worked there for a few years before jumping
to Apple in 1989 as a senior manager.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:37.040
Unfortunately, his tenure at Apple was during
the worst time period for Apple.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.890
Maybe this was one of the reasons Shantanu
was able to score a high-level management

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.409
position though, so it might’ve been a good
thing.

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:50.170
Shantanu worked at Apple up until 1995 when
he left to become a director at silicon graphics.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.909
This didn’t last too long though, and Shantanu
would find himself creating his own startup

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:55.530
during the dot-com bubble.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.659
Shantanu and some of his colleagues picked
up on the rapid trend towards digital photography.

00:02:59.659 --> 00:03:03.749
So, in 1996, they started a business called
Pictra.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:08.370
Pictra allowed customers to share their photos
online similar to Facebook and Instagram.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:13.280
However, after 12 to 14 months in the business,
the team realized that the market for an image-sharing

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.610
website wasn’t that mature and it was extremely
hard to monetize the venture.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.819
So, they decided to try to sell the company
to a giant in the creative space: Adobe.

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:28.540
Unfortunately, Adobe didn’t really care
about Pictra, but during the acquisition meeting,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.779
Adobe did become extremely impressed with
Shantanu.

00:03:30.779 --> 00:03:35.150
They wouldn’t offer to acquire Pictra, but
they did offer Shantanu an extremely attractive

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.079
position as senior vice-president of worldwide
product development.

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:43.150
As you would guess, Shantanu ditched Pictra
shortly after and took on the role at Adobe

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.639
in 1998.

00:03:44.639 --> 00:03:49.529
At Adobe, Shantanu’s biggest contribution
before becoming CEO was deal-making.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:55.159
Throughout the late 1990s and early 2000s,
Shantanu closed 350 deals for the company,

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:59.129
mostly with media firms like Viacom, CBS,
and PBS.

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:04.080
This is why basically every media and gaming
website in the world used Adobe Flash in the

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.499
late 2000s and early 2010s.

00:04:06.499 --> 00:04:10.249
Given Shantanu’s incredible ability to close
lucrative deals, I don’t think you’d be

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:14.029
surprised to hear that he was promoted to
COO in 2005.

00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:19.090
Just two years later in 2007, Bruce Chizen
the CEO of Adobe at the time announced that

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:24.290
he would be stepping down, and this allowed
Shantanu to become the CEO of Adobe in December

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:25.970
of 2007.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.700
At the time, Adobe was in a rather awkward
position.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.870
The company stock had gone sideways for 7
years and it was about to get crushed by the

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.930
2008 financial crisis.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:40.390
On top of this, Adobe had a major piracy problem
on its hands and Adobe flash was about to

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:41.390
get squashed.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:45.350
So, Shantanu had to revive Adobe for the new
mobile era, and his first step in accomplishing

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.690
this was to making Adobe a subscription business.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.720
Back in the day, Adobe came out with a new
Photoshop and a new Premiere Pro every year.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.730
You had versions like Photoshop 2006 and Photoshop
2008.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:01.900
Adobe sold these versions as one-time purchases
that consumers could buy for a few hundred

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.870
dollars each and use for life.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.390
The problem though was that most of these
upgrades were rather small and incremental,

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:12.990
and Adobe only came out with a major overhaul
once every 3 to 5 years.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:16.930
Given how expensive the products were, most
customers simply skipped the incremental upgrades

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.860
and only bought the major overhauls which
meant that Adobe only made revenue from each

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.140
customer once every few years.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:28.290
Shantanu addressed this shortfall by making
all of Adobe’s creative software a subscription.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.740
Most professional customers embraced this
change as it actually made more sense for

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.230
them as well.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:38.580
Instead of dropping $500 on Photoshop and
$500 on Premiere Pro and $500 on After Effects

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.540
every three years, creative professionals
could pay $50 per month to have the latest

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.050
software from Adobe at all times.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.161
On the other hand, many people complained
about having to $50 per month, and many of

00:05:49.161 --> 00:05:52.020
you guys may think that this is way too much.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.690
In reality, it’s not your fault for thinking
that and it’s Adobe’s fault for charging

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:57.950
that much.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:01.570
This simply means that you’re not part of
the target market that Adobe’s targeting.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:06.200
So, Shantanu fought piracy by making the revenue
that Adobe did make from professionals more

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.310
consistent and reliable.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:12.930
At the same time though, Adobe didn’t make
it that much harder to pirate their software,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.040
and this was intentional.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.670
Adobe is a massive tech company, and they
could easily make their software unpirateable

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.130
if they wanted to.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:25.680
I mean it literally took the FBI 13 years
to simply crack the iPhone’s passcode lock.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.440
So, I’d be willing to bet that Adobe’s
21,000 employees could easily make their software

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.630
unpiratable.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:36.560
However, Adobe knows that everyone who is
able to pay for the software does pay for

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:37.560
it.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.540
Here’s the thing, Adobe’s entire customers
base is creatives who know firsthand how hard

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.740
it is to create and run a business.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.230
And while some of these creatives may complain
about Adobe’s pricing, I’d be willing

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:51.770
to bet that 98% of creative professionals
who can afford Adobe software do indeed pay

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.340
for it myself included.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:59.640
Now, of course, there’s a 2% who are unethical
and immoral, but for the most part, creatives

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.670
are willing to pay Adobe $600 per year to
legally get the software that makes their

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.780
entire career possible.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:10.370
With that being said, making Adobe software
unpiratable wouldn’t really increase Adobe’s

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:11.370
revenue.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.470
However, it would cut off up-and-coming creatives
from even trying the software.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:20.280
So, I think Adobe strategically made their
software difficult to pirate but not impossible.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.080
And I think this is perfectly showcased in
how they address pirated software.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.820
A few years ago, Adobe launched something
called Adobe Software integrity service.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:32.730
This software comes along with all of Adobe’s
creative apps and is able to detect if the

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.120
software you’re using is pirated.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.320
If it is pirated, every time you open premiere
pro or Photoshop, it’ll tell you that the

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.370
software is pirated.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.650
But guess what, it doesn’t actually prevent
you from using the application.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:49.560
It simply reminds you every time that you’re
using pirated software.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.530
And I think this approach embodies Shantunu’s
entire tenure as CEO and why he’s such an

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:55.740
admired leader.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.020
Shantanu doesn’t lead by force, he leads
by integrity and transparency.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.710
Adobe could disable the pirated software from
being used right when they detect that it’s

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.010
not genuine.

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:09.790
Or even if they don’t want to do that, they
could at very least run ads on pirated software

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.620
to partially monetize it.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.460
But they don’t even do that.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.230
They simply notify users that they know that
it’s pirated software, and they try to encourage

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.750
users to pay for Adobe software by appealing
to their morals and ethics.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:26.390
This is the same approach Shantanu uses with
all of his employees.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.770
Nowadays, many tech companies are under the
false impression that they can buy the loyalty

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.220
of their employees.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:37.990
They pay absurd salaries like $200,000, $400,000,
$500,000 per year, yet they’re not able

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:39.999
to hold onto their employees.

00:08:39.999 --> 00:08:42.229
Take a look at someone like Mark Zuckerberg.

00:08:42.229 --> 00:08:47.050
He pays his employees extremely well and gives
them every amenity you can think of.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.249
The median salary itself at Facebook is $240,000
per year.

00:08:51.249 --> 00:08:56.470
Yet, Facebook also had the highest turnover
rate in Silicon Valley until this year.

00:08:56.470 --> 00:09:01.480
Meanwhile, the median salary at Adobe is $134,000
per year.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:06.080
This isn’t a low salary by any means, but
it’s also not absurdly high like their tech

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.009
neighbors across the street.

00:09:08.009 --> 00:09:12.970
Adobe also doesn’t have free restaurants
on campus and they don’t give their employees

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:17.689
company cars, but what they do give their
employees is absolute freedom.

00:09:17.689 --> 00:09:20.350
There’s no clocking in or clocking out.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:23.560
There’s no manager nitpicking everything
you do.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.399
And there’s no one forcing you to even do
anything.

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:31.439
Shantanu believes that great employees are
self-motivated and that their output is far

00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:34.100
superior to anything that could be forced
out of them.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:38.829
Now of course, if you abuse this freedom and
slack off, you’re gonna get fired.

00:09:38.829 --> 00:09:42.199
But, employees who use this freedom responsibly
love it.

00:09:42.199 --> 00:09:46.939
In fact, most of the reviews on Glassdoor
and Indeed cite workplace culture as the best

00:09:46.939 --> 00:09:48.920
thing about Adobe.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:52.730
And considering all of this, I don’t think
you’d be surprised to hear that Adobe ranks

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:58.139
#2 in North America for best company culture,
only beaten out by Google.

00:09:58.139 --> 00:10:02.459
And as Shantanu expected, this insane performance
in the employee and customer satisfaction

00:10:02.459 --> 00:10:05.839
categories has carried over to Adobe’s financials.

00:10:05.839 --> 00:10:11.220
When Shantanu became CEO in 2007, Adobe was
profiting about $500 million per year.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.970
Today, they’re profiting $5 billion per
year.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:20.959
The company has consequently grown from a
$20 billion company to a $320 billion company.

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:24.059
And that’s how Shantanu Narayen blew up
Adobe while staying in the good graces of

00:10:24.059 --> 00:10:27.309
his customers, employees, and investors.

00:10:27.309 --> 00:10:31.279
Do you wish your boss was like Shantanu or
if you’re in a leadership role are you like

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:32.279
Shantanu?

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:33.589
Comment that down below.

00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:37.550
Also, drop a like if you think all leaders
should lead like Shantanu.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:41.329
And of course, consider joining our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:45.709
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

